NNAAMMEE
       pbmtoepson - convert a PBM image into Epson printer graphics


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppbbmmttooeeppssoonn

       [--ddppii==_n] [--pprroottooccooll=={eessccpp99|eessccpp}] [--aaddjjaacceenntt] [--nnoonnaaddjjaacceenntt]

       [_p_b_m_f_i_l_e]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmttooeeppssoonn  reads  a  PBM image as input and produces a stream of Epson
       printer graphics as output.

       The input is from the file identified by the _p_b_m_f_i_l_e  argument  or,  if
       you  don't specify _p_b_m_f_i_l_e, from Standard Input.  Output is to Standard
       Output.

       The output is for traditional (ca 1991) Epson 9-wire dot matrix  (some‐
       times  called  ESC/P  9-wire)  printers or newer ESC/P printers.  For a
       more modern Epson ESC/P2 type printer, try ppbbmmttooeessccpp22.

       Before Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004), ppbbmmttooeeppssoonn  could  not  produce  ESC/P
       streams -- only ESC/P 9-wire.

       The  Epson printer protocols are described in Epson's protocol specifi‐
       cation.

       Note that there is no epsontopbm tool - this transformation is one way.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In addition to the options common to all programs  based  on  libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppbbmmttooeeppssoonn recognizes the
       following command line options:




       --pprroottooccooll=={eessccpp99|eessccpp}
              This determines which Epson printer protocol  the  output  uses.
              eessccpp99  is  the  older  ESC/P  9-pin protocol.  eessccpp is the newer
              ESC/P protocol.  For the even newer EESSCC//PP22 protocol, you have to
              use ppbbmmttooeessccpp22 instead.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004).



       --ddppii==_n This  specifies  the  horizontal print density in dots per inch.
              The protocol allows only certain values: 60, 72,  80,  90,  120,
              144, and 240.  Actually, the ESC/P protocol allows a few others,
              but ppbbmmttooeeppssoonn doesn't know how to generate the command  streams
              that use them.

              If you don't specify this, ppbbmmttooeeppssoonn chooses a horizontal print
              density for you consistent with your other options.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004).


       --aaddjjaacceenntt

       --nnoonnaaddjjaacceenntt
              These options determine whether the output  uses  "adjacent  dot
              printing" or not, whatever that is.

              If  you  don't  specify  this,  ppbbmmttooeeppssoonn  selects adjacent dot
              printing unless that is incompatible with your other options.

              This option was new in Netpbm 10.23 (July 2004).




